            "Osteoporosis is bone of normal composition but reduced quantity and quality, porous and brittle, that breaks under loads normal bone would bear — a <strong>fragility fracture</strong> is one that follows a fall from standing height or less. It is silent until the wrist breaks in a fall, a vertebra crushes (sudden back pain, or none at all — just loss of height, a stooped kyphosis and a protuberant abdomen) or the hip fractures. The blood tests — calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase — are <strong>normal</strong>, which distinguishes it from osteomalacia and bone disease of the kidney.",
            "The risks are those that lower peak bone mass or speed its loss: age; female sex and the <strong>menopause</strong> (early or surgical menopause especially); family history of hip fracture; low body weight; smoking and alcohol; inactivity and prolonged immobility; low calcium and vitamin D; <strong>corticosteroids</strong> (7.5 mg or more of prednisolone for three months or longer); rheumatoid arthritis; thyroid excess; hypogonadism; malabsorption; chronic kidney and liver disease; some anti-epileptics; HIV and some antiretrovirals; depot medroxyprogesterone (reversible); and a previous fragility fracture, which doubles the risk of the next. <strong>DEXA</strong> reports a T-score: −1 to −2.5 is osteopenia, −2.5 or lower is osteoporosis; plain X-rays show bone loss only once about a third has gone. Where DEXA is unavailable, a fragility fracture in a postmenopausal woman or a man over 75 is itself the diagnosis and the reason to treat.",
